WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.630
Before getting into the algorithmic details
of the genome assembly, allow

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:10.500
me to introduce a crazy, yet similar
problem to

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.860
the genome assembly problem.
It is the newspaper explosion problem.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:22.080
We have multiple copies of the same New
York Times newspaper.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.710
We put them on a pile of dynamite and
trigger

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.210
the explosion.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:31.920
But please do not do it at home, this is
just hypothetical.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.363
The explosion tears the newspaper into
pieces.

00:00:38.363 --> 00:00:45.230
Some of these pieces got burnt completely.
Some of these big, got burnt

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:51.950
just partially.
A question: what did the newspaper say?

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.610
Well, in order to reconstruct the
newspaper from these

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:03.210
remaining pieces, there's a very important
intuition behind.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:10.670
We use the overlapping information to
construct the newspaper.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.950
In this picture, you see there are two
pieces,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.460
and we can see that they have overlapping
information.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.320
So, we merge these two pieces into one
bigger piece and

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.300
continue the process.
If you are lucky, then you are

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.240
going to see the newspaper, and the
content of the New York

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:38.350
Times in June the 27th is about the human

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:43.675
genome.
Now, I am ready to describe

00:01:43.675 --> 00:01:51.842
the genome sequencing and assembly in the
language of the newspaper explosion problem.

00:01:51.842 --> 00:01:57.630
Similar to the newspaper explosion
problem, when we have multiple copies of

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:03.659
the same newspaper, here we also have
multiple copies of the same genome.

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:10.130
In this slide, you may count that we have
four copies,

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:16.100
but in reality we have millions of copies.
Also, similar

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:22.110
to the process that the
explosion tears the newspaper into pieces,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.770
we also fragment the DNA into smaller
fragments.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:34.190
These short fragments are called reads.
Some reads disappear,

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:43.510
and the task is: from these remaining
pieces, reconstruct the original genome.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:48.180
Remember, there is no positional
information available.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:57.210
So, a way to solve this puzzle is to apply
the same intuition as

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:03.340
you solved the newspaper explosion puzzle:
Use the overlapping information.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:09.474
If you see two reads, here, they have
an overlapping fragment,

00:03:09.474 --> 00:03:15.994
then you reconcile these pieces
into

00:03:15.994 --> 00:03:20.590
a single piece and move further.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:24.920
So this is the overall picture of the
genome sequencing and assembly.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.210
You have multiple copies of the genome.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:32.540
The sequencing machine generates reads, and
the task of the genome

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:38.020
assembly is: from these reads, 
reconstruct the original genome.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:46.140
What makes genome sequencing so difficult?
Genome is like a

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:51.980
book written in the alphabet of
nucleotides, A, T, C, G.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.170
But its length is 3 billion.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:01.050
But unlike we read a book, a modern
sequencing machine cannot read

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.630
from the start to the end of "this book",
but instead they

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:09.250
can only shred the genome and generate
short reads.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:17.430
So, some people think that the genome
puzzle is actually a giant puzzle.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.650
But please remember this one is not
similar to

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:26.280
the normal jigsaw puzzle, where pieces do
not overlap.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:31.240
In the genome puzzle, because reads
overlap,

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.295
so this should be called an overlapping
puzzle.

